  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 320 PM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nCurrently, strong mid level S/WV trough and associated sfc low\nlifting easterly across the Northern Plains this afternoon. \nExtensive cloud cover over western and central North Dakota \nresults, along with a few areas of light rain developing over \nthe past couple of hours along the ND/Montana border and across\nmy north central (trace to few tenths moisture also observed). \nSfc low centered over the Western Devils Lake basin region \ncurrently, results in the well-advertised gusty westerly winds \nover the southwest and south central. Current wind headlines \nlook good, thus no changes at this time. \n\nStrong westerly winds will continue to expand north and east \nthis evening as the sfc low develops northeast into far southern\nManitoba by 06Z. BUFKIT analysis puts the stronger wind gust\npotential now through roughly 02-03Z, then will diminish from \nwest to east thereafter through Thursday morning as the \npressure gradient decreases and a ridge of sfc high pressure \nbuilds into the western Dakotas. As is occurring now, as the \nS/WV swings east-northeast, precipitation will further develop\nacross the state, especially over western and northern areas\nnear the path of this system's 700mb low. QPF amounts generally \nmeasured in the hundreths, with up to a quarter inch of rain \npossible far northwest and north central through tonight.  \n\nWe see a break and calmer weather for Thursday as a S/WV mid\nlevel ridge moves east across the Northern Plains. \n\nSplit flow over the western CONUS Thu night/Fri with initially \nan upper trough over the Desert Southwest and a northern stream\nlow sliding south along the Alberta Rockies. Southwest flow \naloft across the Dakotas during this time as the aforementioned \nS/WV ridge continues east. Models project the base of the \nsouthern stream trough lifting northeast across the Central\nRockies Fri morning, triggering a lee side sfc trough developing\ninto the Dakotas during the day Friday along with increasing\nPOPs. Precipitation chances continue Fri night into Sat, and\nwith colder air being pulled in, some snow is expected to mix in\nor change over to all snow across the west later Friday and \narea wide Fri night and Sat. Little if any accumulations are now\nexpected, with the best QPF to our north and northeast.\n\nThe aforementioned northern stream low becomes quasi-stationary\nover Montana on Saturday, then moves east Sat night/Sun, and \nwill maintain low chances for snow over the northern half of the\nstate this weekend. The main impact of the northern stream low \nwill be much colder daytime highs and nighttime lows as\nreinforced shots of colder air are pulled south from Canada. \nRight now the coldest period is looking to be Sunday and Monday,\nwhen highs are forecast in the low 20s to low 30s, and \novernight lows both Sun and Mon nights are forecast mainly in \nthe teens. Just for fun, wind chill temperatures Mon and Tue \nmornings will approach zero across the north.",
